In article <DBRITCH.95Jan27005434@descartes.super.org>, dbritch@super.org 
(David B. Ritch) writes:
>Has anyone compared the DEC alpha F77 and F90 Fortran compilers?  What
>kind of code do they generate, how fast and robust (or slow and buggy)
>are they?

The generated code should be very similar for the same source input, as
the same code generator is used.  Our goal is "no more than 5% worse"
difference for F77 code compiled with F90 - it usually is a much smaller
difference than that.  (And sometimes F90 is better.)

If you recode algorithms using F90 features, then comparisons are more
difficult.  Depending on how you use the F90 features, you can end up
with a significantly faster program or a significantly slower one.

I'm biased, of course, regarding the robustness of the compilers, so any
comment from me won't be too useful.

Our recommendation is that if you've got F77 code, buy DEC Fortran (the
F77 compiler).  If you want to use F90, buy DEC Fortran 90.  You won't
get significant benefits using the F90 compiler for F77 code.

