In article <3ggqj5$hsg@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, devans+@pitt.edu (Debi Evans) writes:
> I am in a group that has a Dec-3000 alpha system. Just the 
> other
> day we found a TLZ06 tape drive "lyng" about. This is one of 
> thos external tape drives. So I plugged it into the SCSI
> port of the machine, and powered the system up. 
> It seems that the system does not know this device is 
> here. When I type "file /dev/*" it does not show up.
> Is there an easy way to do this properly and then 
> say mount the tape drive. I do not have manuals and the 
> CD-rom is no longer connected , so I can't read this 
> online help.
> 
> Sorry about this basic question. I would read the manuals
> if I had access to them :-(. Oh yeah the system is using
> the OFS unix OS. If anyone can tell me how to do this 
> simply I would be very grateful.
> 
> thanks
> debi
> 
Dear Debi,

	first type "show device" at the console prompt after having shut
down the system with "shutdown -h +15 "Hardware upgrade"". Just set the
SCSI-ID of the tape drive to some number that is still free, i.e. not
used by any disk drive or the controller (usually ID=7). Then the drive
should show up at the proper ID number.
On a OSF/1 system, SCSI disks are known as "rz" devices, whereas SCSI tapes
are known as "tz" devices. You might try out the man pages for "SCSI",
"rz","tz","MAKEDEV".
Let us assume your TLZ06 has the SCSI-ID set to 2. Then the drive will
be "tz2" and will be known to the users as /dev/rmt0h,/dev/nrmt0h,/dev/rmt0l,
/dev/nrmt0l.
As the first step you will need to modify (rebuild) your kernel to
reflect the hardware changes. You should edit the file /sys/conf/<machinename>
and include the line "device tape  tz2  at scsi0  drive 16".
Then copy your old kernel to a backup copy: "cp /vmunix /vmunix.old".
Type "doconfig -c <machinename>" and move the new kernel to the root
directory: "mv /sys/<machinename>/vmunix /vmunix". (<machinename> is the
name in capital letters of your machine, say "SIRIUS"). After rebooting
the system with "shutdown -r +15 "Reboot new kernel", you should be
ready to make the device files for the new hardware item. Go to the
/dev directory and type "MAKEDEV tz2". After this you should end up
with the files /dev/rmt0h...

Good luck.

						Hans Schwengeler

						schwengeler@urz.unibas.ch


