In article <3fii0p$557@pretzel.cs.huji.ac.il>, werman@humus.cs.huji.ac.il (Michael Werman) writes:
> 	We are planning to use an alpha station
> to run a robot and wondered if anyone knows of any problems
> of running real time applications under windows nt using an alpha
> station
> any info would be appreciated.
> 
> thankx
> werman@cs.huji.ac.il

-- 
Hi there, as you will probably be aware the alpha is a RISC machine.
It gains a lot of performance from having multiple pipelines to keep the 
CPU crunching. In order to perform a non-scheduled operation it must waste 
time flushing its pipelines and reloading then with new code.

Of course it all depends on what you mean by 'real time applications'.
Do you mean ON-LINE DATA capture at regular intervals where you can 
calculate and test the CPU response time and guarantee that the next 
interrupt will never happen before you have processed the first.?
Do you mean random events that must be responded to within X microseconds,
because another event may happen at X+1 microseconds later.?
Do you mean real events that are initiated by the processor at given time 
intervals or at specific absolute times.?

Most ROBOT control function can be readily handled with 1 to 10 interrupts
per second - this should be easy for an alpha (even with the windows nt).
If you want the robot to change operations within microseconds of detecting 
a random situation the I would offer the wisdom of one of the alpha design 
engineers at a DECUS symposium a couple of years ago when asked about the 
latency time for 'real time interrupts' which was that "PDP-11s are still 
the recommended systems for real-time work".

