In article <1995Jan20.000037.47@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se>,
anos@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se (Anders Ostling) writes: 
|>I would like to know if anyone has heard if OpenVMS (AXP) will allow
|>booting a cluster satellite over FDDI. We are thinking of adding a new
|>2100 to our existing CI cluster, and since the CI adapter still isn't
|>available for 2100 (CI-PCI), one soultion would be to boot over the LAN
|>link instead.

Yes, OpenVMS AXP supports booting over FDDI.  Your 2100 would need firmware
revision 3.9 to support booting over the EISA-bus FDDI adapter, I'm told.

|>There is one other possibilitly that I would like to check. Could I connect
|>a cable from the ethernet port (10bT) on the 2100 to an unused DEMNA on a
|>DEC-7610 and use that link (in effect, an isolated LAN segment) as boot
|>and system disk "channel". Is it possible ? Are there drawbacks ? Any ideas
|>are welcome.

Yes, you could use an Ethernet LAN segment connected between the two systems as
a way to boot the 2100.  The Ethernet isn't terribly fast, but since you
wouldn't be sharing it with anyone, if the I/O load across it wasn't high, it
might provide acceptable performance.

|>If possible, how do I arrange for booting over ethernet, and then use FDDI
|>as primary LAN port (for DECNET/TCPIP/LAT). Only SCS over ethernet I guess.

You'd specify the Ethernet adapter as the boot device, and run CLUSTER_CONFIG
on the boot node and answer all its questions to allow the satellite to boot. 

Once the system is booted and running OpenVMS, the cluster software
automatically detects all the possible LAN paths (both Ethernet and FDDI), and
the default is for it to enable _all_ of them for use, selecting on transmit
the path with the lowest latency.  If you wanted to turn SCS communications off
for the Ethernet port after booting, you can use the LAVC$STOP_BUS program from
SYS$EXAMPLES: to do that, or else just raise NISCS_MAX_PKTSZ to something above
the size Ethernet can handle, so the cluster software will use FDDI in
preference to Ethernet (but leave the Ethernet enabled for SCS communications
for failover purposes in case the FDDI fails sometime).

Yet another option would be add a KFESA to the 2100 and connect a DSSI bus
between the 2100 and the DEC 7000.

|>Lastly, even if there will be an CI-PCI adapter from DEC later this year, is
|>the FDDI preferable over CI in terms of I/O throughput/bandwith ? Maybe the
|>only advantage is direct access to the HSJ disk farm. 

The CI would presumably have a bit lower CPU overhead, but an Alpha is so fast
you probably wouldn't care.  I think you're right that the major difference is
whether or not you have direct access to the existing CI-based storage.  On the
other hand, Digital now sells FDDI-based storage as well (the FVS30 and the
HS1xx series), so that may be less important in the future. 
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Keith B. Parris, StorageWorks Host-Based Software Team, Digital Equip. Corp.



