If I understand what you're trying to do, you want to set up one or more
DECservers (with modem control) serving up a collection of modems for
access via remote LAT.

Perfectly sane and reasonable thing to do.

And with PATHWORKS, you don't need a dedicated server.

In fact, the only part of PATHWORKS that you use is the LAT capabilities
in the PC _clients_, using either the VT320 emulator (under Windows) or
SETHOST.

Macintosh clients can connect to the modems using the Comm. Toolbox LAT
tool (supplied with PATHWORKS for Macintosh) and MacTerminal (or any Comm.
Toolbox-aware terminal emulator).

You can create a SINGLE service name for all of the DECserver-served
modems, and when you connect to it, the next available modem is connected
to the PC.

If you have people logging into your VMS servers (for interactive use, not
for PATHWORKS file services), they can also access the DECservers if you
enable outgoing LAT on the VMS system.  They can then do $ SET HOST/LAT
commands to access the modem pool.

In short:

Yes, you can do exactly what you want to do.

No, you don't need any dedicated servers.

