> Hello,
> i got this too.
> I don't remember clearly the details. But, is has something to do with
> the order you "start" networking software. "Some" manual stated that
> DECNET demands having lowest, next to cluster driver, numbering on the
> ethernet drivers. You get the above stated problem when XWindows, UCX,
> LAT, or something else is started before the DECNET, and thus "hogging"
> the driver numbers that DECNET want. So, look through your systartup file.
> See that DECNET starts first, then LAT, then UCX, then others.
> 
> Hmm...? I thought DEC solved this by OPENVMS552 ??
> 
> Observe:
> Things stated above are personal fluxuous opinions.
> This posting is just a hint, not a remedy.

What causes this is that DECnet sets up an alternate hardware address that
resembles the DECnet address of the node.  That's the AA-00... address you see
occassionally.  If another ethernet protocol is started before DECnet, then
DECnet can't make this change and gets upset.  Just ensure that DECnet starts
first, and you won't get this error.

