morphis@fnalv1.fnal.gov wrote:
: Hello,
: 	over on Comp.Arch there has been some discussion of what the 
: "typical" PC of 2005 is going to look like, well the discussion started
: there generally went wild with holographic projectors...  but along
: the way the question was asked  What frequency do the current busses in the
: various Alphas run at.  The numbers quoted in the catalogs are in MB/s
: not MHz.
: 	I once compared the measured data flow of a 3600 vs. the numbers
: for the PCI bus obtained by freq*width.  It was pointed out to me that
: freq*width is not the best measure of such things.  So how is the 100MB/s
: number for teh 3700 arrived at and what are the actual frequencies of the
: various busses (TURBO, FUTURE, XMI) that the alphas use?

That question is hard to answer.  The external bus on the Alpha chip is
programmable as to 64 or 128 bit width, and how many cycles to wait for
external cache, etc. to respond.  So the bus can run as fast as the CPU
clock / 2 (if my memory serves), or as slow as you might want, I think
CPU clock / 32.  This is programmed into the chip at power on time by
a serial PROM, with a lot of other configuration info such as external
cache configuration, external bus configuration, virtual page size,
and dozens of other configuration settings.  The external bus can also
run at 3.3 V CMOS levels, or at ECL levels, also configured by the PROM.



