In article <3g0n5h$2b1@hopper.acm.org>, jgreen@ACM.ORG writes...
>I'm trying to read binary data from a STREAM file and write
>it out as a fixed-length record format file.  The problem is,
>the C library functions (or the RMS functions underneath) are
>still interpreting control characters, thus scrambling
>information.  Example

There is an unfortunate naming clash for STREAM files.
For *decades* Digital had used the term STREAM/STM on RSTS/E, RSX and VMS
to mean a very particular format with specific semantics: STREAM-11 / STM-11

Since, the industry, and Unix in particular, has used that 
same word but uses it to describe what on VMS would correspond 
better with STREAM_LF or even UNDEFINED files.

The semantics for STM-11 tell RMS to SKIP leading zeros in records :-(.
This is the way it is defined, so RMS (and the C RTL) obliges. 

Please consider trying STREAM_LF or UNDEFINED files for your work.

