    To say that P/OS is RSX with menus is about 80% correct.
Many RSX images will run without any problems. However, that
20% can really get you. For some reason they decided not to use
RSX's command line dispatching mechanism, but instead something
that I believe is based on the Send/Receive directives. GMCR$
works fine, but none of the others (eg: RPOI$) do. On the other
hand, PACman-11 runs just fine -- I've even got it installed on
a menu. 

    I have heard about two-user P/OS, though rumor has it that
it was a little strange (eg: if one user changed defaults, the
other was dragged along willy-nilly). I would not count on
three-user. The comm port does not use the TT: driver (which is
the interactive terminal driver), but something called the XK:
driver. I rather doubt that it has all the interactive user
support that the TT: driver has. In the immortal words of Bruce
Mitchell, the TT: driver is "a mouse and a half", and I suspect
that two such would be one too many.

    There are also, if you get down to this level, a couple of
piddly differences in the disk formatting (all P/OS directories
are owned by [200,200] for example). Floppies are
interchangable with any other machine that supports FILES-11
(meaning, PDP-11s running RSX and VAXen running VMS), but you
will get some minor gripes from the OS. Nothing to worry about.

						-- Tom Wyant


