> That question is hard to answer.  The external bus on the Alpha chip is
> programmable as to 64 or 128 bit width, and how many cycles to wait for
> external cache, etc. to respond.  So the bus can run as fast as the CPU
> clock / 2 (if my memory serves), or as slow as you might want, I think
> CPU clock / 32.  This is programmed into the chip at power on time by
> a serial PROM, with a lot of other configuration info such as external
> cache configuration, external bus configuration, virtual page size,
> and dozens of other configuration settings.  The external bus can also
> run at 3.3 V CMOS levels, or at ECL levels, also configured by the PROM.

At least on the 21064 and 21064A, the bus speeds are configured by how
the interrupt requests are pulled at reset. For ECL/CMOS there's a
separate pin, I believe.
