In article <3fsskh$qnd@bigfoot.wustl.edu>,
Jonathan M. Elson <jmelson@artsci.wustl.edu> wrote:
>: Just how far out in left field is this drive?
>
>All the way out.  It's a full custom drive, basically a DEC RA81 without
>the SDI interface. It has no reason to look like SMD except that it is
>a voice coil driven multiplatter disk drive, so at some level the signals
>are probably similar to those in an SMD interface just because the SMD
>interface is about as basic as you can get.

Careful.  It sounds like you're talking about the R80 drive, which is the
guts of the RM80 and RA80 (not RA81), and can be connected directly to the
RB730 controller on a VAX 730.  It is indeed totally non-standard, but in
the RM80 it has an SMD interface stuck onto it, so that it can talk to the
"RM Adapter" SMD-to-Massbus interface, which is the rack o' logic that fits
under an RM02, RM03, or RM80 drive, or next to an RM05.  Supposedly the
SMD spoken by the RM adapter is in some way non-standard, but no one seems
to know in exactly what way.  But the rumor goes on to say that you get
smoke if you plug a generic SMD drive (i.e. none of the above) in, which
makes me hesitant to try it myself.  If I could find a bunch more DK512s
I'd be willing to risk one of them...

What an unreal setup...  R80, SMD interface, RM Adapter, Massbus interface,
Unibus adapter, and finally main memory.  No wonder it draws so much power!
And the SMD interface is the only thing with a brain, at least it lets
you key in hex commands for self-test and so on (you can leave it randomly
scribbling on the last two cyls for hours w/no computer plugged in).

John Wilson


