In article <3fsskh$qnd@bigfoot.wustl.edu>, jmelson@artsci.wustl.edu (Jonathan M. Elson) writes:
> Tim Thompson (tigger@solaria.sol.net) wrote:
> : Has anyone ever attemped (or, more importantly, succeeded) to attach
> : an RM80 drive to a standard (non-DEC) SMD controller?
> 
> : Just how far out in left field is this drive?
> 
> All the way out.  It's a full custom drive, basically a DEC RA81 without
> the SDI interface. It has no reason to look like SMD except that it is
> a voice coil driven multiplatter disk drive, so at some level the signals
> are probably similar to those in an SMD interface just because the SMD
> interface is about as basic as you can get.  SMD does seeking by sending the
> cylinder address in parallel binary to the drive and giving a seek signal.

  Are you sure about this? The R80 is the basic drive with no personality
module. This is used in the VAX 11/730 with the RB730, a combo RL02/R80 con-
troller. The RA80 adds an SDI personality module. I believe the RM80 adds a
SMD personality module, since I've seen RM80's connected to the Massbus via
Massbus adaptors. In fact, I think Tim's RM80 started out in life like this.

  DEC tended to do weird stuff in their RM drives. For example, the RM05, an
OEM'd CDC 9766, did dual-port in the adaptor, not the drive, and I think it
also hard-coded some stuff in the drive interface cards that was usually
read from the controller. I do know you need to swap a couple of the drive's
interface cards to use them on generic SMD controllers - stuff beyond the
usual "clocks on A or B cable" things.

