arog@BIX.com (arog on BIX) writes:

>POS went to a Release 3 before it died. As I understand it, 
>there are a couple of objections to the last version. First, 
>and probaly formost, is the report that it insists on the
>menu system for most every thing. 

  P/OS is essentially menus on top of RSX.  But even under P/OS 3.2 (the
  last official release) DCL and MCR command-line-interpreters are available
  as an alternative to the menu system.  VAX/VMS users would find that
  the PRO's DCL is very familiar, though not as full-featured as VMS DCL.
  I have my account set up to go straight to DCL when I log in, bypassing
  the menu system entirely.

  Some crude window-based utilities are also available with the P/OS 3.2
  kit, known collectively as "Synergy" -- these include window manager, file
  browser, calendar, spreadsheet, graph maker, calculator, data manager, and
  PROSE Plus word processor.  The software is all quite antiquated by
  today's standards, but works as well as it ever did.

  P/OS V3 has quite a number of nice features that V2 didn't have.  It
  supports subdirectories, a real standalone backup/restore utility (PROBRU),
  username/password accounts, shared libraries (install an application once
  and all users share the same copy), etc.

  - Kurt


