In article <D2KBBM.8Mq@sybase.com>, elyes@sybase.com  writes:
>Hi 
>
>Could anyone, please, tell me if the VMS limitation on a process name 
>to 14 characters exist on OSF/1 too ? 

I sent this in mail to the author, but with the confusion going on
in the follow-ups I've seen, it is worth a bit of explaination.

Short answer: OSF/1 doesn't a process name.

In VMS each process has a name as well as a process ID.  The name
of the program being run within process is (if I remember my VMS
correctly) the image.  It is distinct from the process name.  At
some level the process name must be unique.  The documentation
leads me to believe that it unique at the system level, but
experimentation suggests that it is only unique on a per
user basis.

For the commands that affect processes, the name can be used
to identify the process as well as the ID.  A default name
is assigned when the process is created, but it can be changed
with the SET PROCESS/NAME= command.

This particular feature of process identification doesn't exist
on OSF (or any UNIX that I know of).

