bronson@aquilagroup.com (Mark Bronson) writes:

>Can anyone recommend a benchmark that would indicate a platform's ability
>to perform complex FFT?

There are C code FFT's available. Just look on the Benchmark repositories.

However, almost any benchmark fft you see quoted will be a hand-written
assembly language version. Typically these will be a few times faster than
a C version. FFT's are one of those benchmarks people who want to show
a chip off to advantage always hand-code, so if you are looking at DSP chips
(for example), I would expect the manufacturers to be able to supply some code.

Unless you are running the same C code on all your processors, you can't
compare any figures you get with any quoted figures. And if you do run the
C fft on all the processor's of interest the numbers you get will have marginal
correlation with how fast each chip can actually do an FFT since if
you really need spedacious (new word) FFT's you will hand-code them..



