$500 for a stripped 3100 is OK -- not great.  You can drop standard 
SCSI-I disks inside, two MAX if you keep the RX23 drive as well.  You may 
need to fashion a cable to provide connectors for two SCSI drives or buy 
the correct on from DEC for a paltry (gulp!) $130 or so.  If you only 
need one SCSI drive, the cable should already be in it.

There were several models of 3100: 10, 20, 10e, 20e, 30 and probably 
more.  I wish I could remember the VUP rating: 2.8 and 3.7, I think.  
Maybe someone else can fill that detail in.

If you want VMS legally you'll have to pay around $300 for a license 
transfer.  Make sure the seller gives you PAKs (paper licenses) for all 
the products.  You should get one for VMS and DECnet.  C won't legally 
transfer, DECnet is a maybe.  DEC used to considered it "bundled" but I 
think that may have changed.  Perhaps someone can post a response to that 
issue.  Lanworks (Pathworks for Macintosh) server license is free with VMS.

VMS V5.2 is quite old so the other products are probably as well.  How 
will you get current media?  DEC will stick it to you for current rev 
media.

As you can see, the "incidentals" could run a pile 'o money if you play 
it legally.

