But I disagree with this statement.  If you are doing the same
work in a well-tuned hand-coded way on two different processors,
I say that's *exactly* what you want to compare.

Who cares if it's not the same code?  This is the approach NAS
and PARKBENCH have taken.  NAS takes the attitude that you can
just program the benchmark yourself (they provide a uniprocessor
template).  PARKBENCH intends to distribute baseline codes that
you run as-is.  You may also run a fully-optimised (customized) 
version of the benchmark, too, provided that you specify what
you did.

(Granted, both these suites are for parallel machines.  But with
today's DSP chips, I think the application of the methodology is 
a logical one.)

