Tim Thompson (tigger@solaria.sol.net) wrote:


: Has anyone ever attemped (or, more importantly, succeeded) to attach
: an RM80 drive to a standard (non-DEC) SMD controller?

: Just how far out in left field is this drive?

All the way out.  It's a full custom drive, basically a DEC RA81 without
the SDI interface. It has no reason to look like SMD except that it is
a voice coil driven multiplatter disk drive, so at some level the signals
are probably similar to those in an SMD interface just because the SMD
interface is about as basic as you can get.  SMD does seeking by sending the
cylinder address in parallel binary to the drive and giving a seek signal.
The RM80 might use a step and direction line like ST506 drives, or serial
commands like ESDI to save wires in the cable.  I haven't looked at 
schematics.  The real question is : Why would you want to do this?  It would
take months of tinkering to get it working, and you can get much better
(better MTBF, quieter, faster, less power consumption and heat) drives for 
only $300 - 500 now!  Why knock yourself out?



