        Err...not to question someone employed  by Digital, but the last
    statement  I  underlined  is patently false.  It may be  done  as  a
    matter of policy in some quarters (always giving  SPEC  numbers  for
    "UNIX"),  but  the  SPEC  benchmark  suite  is,  as far as I know, a
    _system_  benchmark  and   therefore   is  done/given  for  whatever
    operating  system  (and  compiler versions and  switches)  is  being
    benchmarked (and I won't get  into  the  question  of  _which_  UNIX
    you're referring to, nor the assertion that OpenVMS _is_ UNIX :-).

        Furthermore, the original poster  (Jerry)  was asking about SPEC
    numbers  _specifically_  for OpenVMS.  How can you tell him that  he
    needs to ignore the (published) OpenVMS SPEC numbers and  look  only
    at the UNIX (well, OSF/1) numbers???

        The only other point is that it  is well known that the OVMS and
    OSF/1   SPEC  numbers  differ  for  the  same  hardware,   sometimes
    significantly and sometimes not.  This  is  due  both  to  "compiler
    creep"  as  new  versions  of  the various compilers are released at
    different times for the different  operating  systems, and due to at
    least  one  of  the integer benchmarks being  heavily  dependent  on
    curses, which is not optimal on OVMS...

