Hi Folks:

        We're upgrading our GKS/VWS GUI to X/Motif.  I know, it's about time!
Anyway, I'm developing a Menu Bar in which I want to provide an "Access Menu"
with a list of the CPUs in the cluster.  When a user selects a menu item,
I want to create a DECterm window connected to the machine they want.
With the GKS/VWS system they have now, they have to create a VT200 window
by selecting a menu option on the background menu, login to the workstation,
then "set host cp0_0_" ==> where the underscores map to CPU# and Cluster#,
and login again.  Users said they wish they didn't have to login so much.
We want to make life easier for them.

        Some caveats:

        o  X/Motif is only on the VAXstation 3100, NOT the CPUs.

        o  I cannot create DECterm on the CPUs and export the display to
           the VAXstation.  The overhead of making that happen could cause
           a blip in the CPU performance when lots of people are calling
           an 800 number to order Ginsu Knives!!!  As a result, some calls
           could get dropped and they may not call back to order.


	I've had the DECwTermPort() and sys$creprc() part working for weeks.
Someone recommended creating a .COM file to do the "set host" work, and
specifying the .COM file name in a descriptor passed to sys$creprc() in
the 3rd field (input).  But there's no evidence that the .COM ever gets
executed.  DECterm always prompts me at the machine I'm running on.

	I added a few lines to create a .DAT file from the .COM, just to
leave a "footprint" when the .COM is run; but the .DAT file isn't created.
Here's what the .COM does:

=============================================================================
CP0104: type GOTO_MC0109.COM
$! -- connect to MC0104
$ set nover
$ set noon
$!
$! Leave a footprint when this .COM is run.
$!
$ define sys$output beavis.dat
$ show default
$ deassign sys$output
$!
