The PRO-350 can address 1 meg Word (2 meg bytes). Base memory is
on two daughter boards attached directly to the main board and
added memory is on the 'buss' with normally one 128K memory board.

The daughter boards can be re-populated with 512K chips, two jumpers
each added, and will then be logged on boot. Added bus memory boards
auto configure.

Slot one should be the RX-50 controller, two should be a fixed disk,
three, the first added memory card. 

Video, if one card, is the monocrome, if two cards with a ribbon
cable over the top, is the color configureation. 

With RX-50 controller, Fixed disk, one memory card, and the cards for
color, there should be one empty slot. 

There were a couple of versions of  the Fixed Disk controller.
The early versions could deal with ST-506 (5 meg) and the 10 meg
version. There is an article in one of the DEC Micro Journals
that details how to build a POS boot disk that will format and
install an ST-225 for the full 20 megs under POS R 2.0.

This controller CANNOT deal with either the Quantum 540 (32 meg)
or 64 
or 64 meg Micropolis drives. A later version can use those drives,
but,  (as I recall) there is a jumper that must be added to the
Microp for it to be DEC_Compatable. 

The PRO-380 uses a more recent PDP-11 CPU, a color card that is
a daughter board, memory daughter boards that already have the
512k chips, and a couple of other changes. 

POS went to a Release 3 before it died. As I understand it, 
there are a couple of objections to the last version. First, 
and probaly formost, is the report that it insists on the
menu system for most every thing. 

A second and perhaps third user can be added by using the serial
and printer port (this is distant recolection here) for VT-100
terminals. 

There, did that help?

