|> processors if the data do not fit well into their various
|> cache configurations. [...]
|> Summary, the DEC and SGI can't sustain memory bandwidths of
|> much more than ~100 MB/sec. The HP 735/99 is about 80 MB/sec.
|> 
|> The IBM RS6000 500 series POWER2 boxes can sustain up to 600 MB/sec
|> and can therefore keep the FP processors busier. [..]
|> For big 3D problems, the DEC, HP, SGI boxes don't perform nearly
|> as well as the RS6000 500 (or 900) POWER2 series.

There are also algorithms which do not access memory very regularly.
For them, a large memory bandwidth is not such an important factor
as for the above benchmark.  Rather, the processor must be able
to cope reasonably well with frequent cache misses.

An example is a molecular dynamics benchmark that you can find on
the Web at http://www.sissa.it/furio/mdbnch.html .
Present results indicate, for the running times,

DEC 2100/400 275MHz, OSF/1 3.0, f77 3.5, -O4 .................. 22.0 s
SGI PwrChallenge, R8000(75 MHz),f77(MIPSpro 6.0) -mips4 -64 -O3 23.8 s
IBM R/6000 590 (67.0 MHz) [^] -Pk -qarch=pwrx -qnosave ........ 30.7 s
HP 9000/735 (99 Mhz), f77 (9.00) +O3 +OP4 +OPunroll ........... 33.0 s

[note that a 125 MHz version of the HP 735 also exists; an estimate 
 time for it could be, by scaling the clocks, about 26 s].
Therefore, the situation is exactly the opposite than in the Streams
benchmark, with IBM being here the slowest of the four.

