(Todd K. Pedlar) writes:

|>	I'm a bit new to the system management thing, and I've got 
|>a question that should be easily answerable by the gurus of the group...
|>we've got an AlphaStation 400 which was purchased without the software
|>distribution, but of course with the relevant licenses - so, in order
|>to get things going, I have two options as I see it:
|>
|> - I can install the OpenVMS system
|>and software on the system disk, boot as standalone, then join
|>the cluster later
|>
|> - I can boot the AlphaStation as a satellite of the two Alpha 3000-400's
|>we now have clustered, and later make the AlphaStatin the boot node of the
|>cluster.
|>
|>I've tried the second option, but have not been able to make it work.  Is 
|>there a limitation on the OpenVMS system I'm running currently?  I've got
|>6.2 on order, but for the momewe're still running 1.5.  If so, is this 
|>going to negate my other option as well?


    OpenVMS Alpha V1.5 predates much of the Alpha hardware presently
    available, including the entire AlphaStation series.  Specifically,
    you'll need a minimum of V6.1-1H1 for the AlphaStation 400...

    If you purchased the AlphaStation with factory-configurec disk(s),
    you may/should have a factory-installed copy of OpenVMS present on
    the disk.

    If you do not have a factory-installed or a distribution copy of
    OpenVMS Alpha V6.1-1H1, you'll need to obtain a copy of the
    distribution and install it on the AlphaStation and/or on the
    VMScluster.  Both installation procedures are a little longer
    than I'd prefer to type here -- they are documented in the OpenVMS
    System Management and the Cluster Management manuals.

    If you decide to add the AlphaStation to the VMScluster, you'll
    want to run V6.1 or V6.1-1H1 on all VMScluster nodes, and you'll
    want to  check for the appropriate console PALcode for *all*
    VMScluster Alpha nodes *before* upgrading the VMScluster -- if
    you've got systems running V1.5, you probably also got PALcode
    of a similar (old) vintage -- PALcode which may not be compatible
    with more recent OpenVMS releases.

