|> as well as the RS6000 500 (or 900) POWER2 series. The memory
|> tends to cost more because of the number of banks required.

Both the STREAMS benchmark and your conclusions from it ignore one
critical characteristic of a cache -- its size.

The SGI/MIPS R8000 has a cache of 4 MB, while the 
IBM RS6000 cache is 256 KB.  In my benchmarking experience with
real-life codes, the machines are in the same ballpark, i.e., 
having a cache that's 16x larger just about balances out having 
cache misses that are 5x faster, and vice versa.  
This is on problems that are much bigger than either cache.  
 
|> 
|> John D. McCalpin recently posted his testing results for the Cray
|> J90 vs the 8 processor PowerChallenge. He found the single processor
|> Cray J90 had 10 times the memory bandwidth of the 8 processor 
|> PowerChallenge.

Using STREAMS to compare these two machines is also flawed.

The Cray must go to main memory for virtually all of its 
memory references, so STREAMS is typical of how that 
machine will be used.

A cache-based machine like the Power Challenge goes to the 
memory bus only on cache misses, so STREAMS is representative
of worst-case performance, which may or may not be typical 
of your application.   In my experience, STREAMS is not an 
accurate predictor of "typical" performance even on large 3d codes.
 
|> 
|> Best thing to do: Try your code on all machines you're seriously
|> considering. Try running a range of problem sizes that's realistic
|> of your anticipated usage pattern.
|> 

Agreed.  And don't assume that programs exhibit bad cache-hit rates
just because the dataset is larger than cache.  

Jack

