dump uses a simple algorithm for estimating tape capacity that is completely
useless for modern tape drives - most of them have a capacity that varies
depending on several paramters, including how quickly data is fed to them
(they will pad blocks to avoid repositioning), how compressable the data is,
and so forth. The dump media capacity calculations assume a simple 9-track
compatible tape drive; unfortunately, this model needs `help' to approximate
the behavior of today's intelligent drives. The parameters that are suggested
for dump are at best compromises - indeed, you can't tell how much tape
capacity is left unless you model the entire behavior of the drive including
compression and padding.

dump, for supported drives, gives a ballpark guess. If you override the
guess with `better' paramters, you are likely limiting the capacity of 
your tapes - when the parameters you specify say the tape is full,
dump assumes it's full. If you let dump alone, it'll write data until the
physical end of tape - usually a better indication of the total media
capacity :-)
