        Sorry, I think you're  p%ss*ng  and moaning over nothing.  Every
    time (substantially) new hardware is released, VMS has to know about
    it.   The  new  machines usually come with a VMS  -nH  type  version
    (e.g., VMS V5.5-2H4).  _Usually_, the changes are really  additional
    _boot_  files  that  know  about  the  hardware  (look  at  all  the
    SYSLOAxxx.EXE  files  in  SYS$COMMON:[SYS$LDR]  on  VAX  hardware...
    they're likely called something else on AXP hardware).

        Now you might ask  your  Digital  support  people if there is an
    interim  way  you could get your new AlphaStations to boot  off  the
    cluster, e.g., by copying the additional "loader" files to the  boot
    server.   Alternatively,  perhaps you could _temporarily_ use one of
    the AlphaStations as the  boot  server, use CLUSTER_CONFIG.COM on it
    to  create a second system disk (whatever the menu item says...)  on
    the boot server, and then switch the boot  server  to  use  the  new
    system disk.  [No, I wouldn't want to do it that way either...  :-)]

        Finally,  there's   nothing   keeping   you   from  booting  the
    AlphaStations  from their local system disk and having them join the
    cluster.  This is supported: you don't need to boot satellites  over
    the  network  if yo don't want to (unless they're diskless :-).  You
    may have improved  performance,  in  fact,  since system images will
    come  off the AlphaStation's local disk rather than over the network
    from the boot server.  You just need to  set  up  logical  names  in
    SYLOGICALS.COM   to   point   SYSUAF,   RIGHTSLIST,  NET$PROXY  (and
    NETPROXY),   VMSMAIL_PROFILE,    and    perhaps    SYS$SYLOGIN   and
    NETNODE_REMOTE,  at the cluster common version of those files (along
    with the corresponding  commands  to  mount  the  disks  where  they
    reside).   Later, when the new VMS version arrives on the CD and you
    upgrade the cluster, you can boot the AlphaStations over the net and
    be done with it...
 
