In article <D29G7C.76w@has.nl>, klix@has.nl (Peter Klix - Has Computer Systems)
writes:
|>Does somebody have experience with the new SCSI clustering facility that comes
|>with OpenVMS 6.2 and does someone know's what the restrictions are ?

Based on the information handed out at the U.S. DECUS Symposium in Anaheim, the
requirements are:
o  All nodes sharing access to storage on a SCSI interconnect must be running
   OpenVMS AXP V6.2 and VMScluster Software
o  AlphaServer 1000, 2000, & 2100 and AlphaStation 200 & 400 systems are
   supported
o  SCSI adapters used for SCSI clusters must be either the integral adapter
   on the above systems or a KZPAA (PCI-bus SCSI adapter)
o  Supported SCSI devices are various disks in the RZ line, & maybe the HSZ40
o  SCSI disk drives can be multi-hosted.  SCSI tape drives cannot.
o  Max. of 3 host CPUs on a SCSI bus (tri-host SCSI cluster)
o  Max. of 2 shared SCSI busses connected to a given host CPU
o  Another cluster interconect (FDDI, DSSI, or Ethernet) must connect the
   systems on a shared SCSI bus (inter-node SCS communications does not go
   over the SCSI bus at this time)
o  Due to device naming issues:
   -  all nodes on a shared SCSI bus must have the same non-zero disk
      allocation class
   -  adapters connected to the same shared SCSI bus must have the same
      controller letter (e.g. DKA or DKB) (so devices will have the same device
      name on all the systems connected to that SCSI bus)
   -  only one SCSI adapter on a given system can be connected to a given
      SCSI bus (or you'd have two sets of device names for the same devices)
o  Bus length between systems is fairly limited, and must take into account
   the internal cabling inside each system.  Differential signalling (with
   the help of the DWZZA converter) can help here.
As this is all pre-release information, it is subject to change.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Keith B. Parris, StorageWorks Host-Based Software Team, Digital Equip. Corp.



