  OK, I can understand why DEC have got themselves in this position.
The fundamental problem seems to be that when DEC just have too many
hardware and software combinations on their Alpha workstations for 
them to support properly. While it's good to have lots of options -
Windows NT, VMS and OSF/1 each on either Turbochannel or PCI based
machines - the downside is that customers get confused about what you're
trying to sell and software support suffers because of the number of
possible combinations.
  A comparison to make is when HP introduced it's 700 series machines
they said "we'll never support Domain OS on these machines - you'll
have to switch to HP/UX". In the short term this made them unpopular
with their existing users but in the longer term it made a lot of
sense. I don't have a magic solution for how DEC can solve this
problem - that's not my job and it's probably too late now anyway.  
  If the Alphastation is indeed "aimed primarily at the PC market"
then the prices need to be cut drastically. At the moment there is
no way these are competitive compared to Pentium based systems.
  
