In <3emv0r$l9q@vunews.valpo.edu>, kenp@valpo.edu (Ken Purczynski) writes:
] I'm trying to install Ultrix 4.4 on a DECstation 3100 using an
] HP2490 disk.  The previous administrator said that she was able
] to install the OS on a third party disk (Fujitsu) without a
] hitch.  

That's the case with me, too.  I have a Fujitsu 500 MB disk and
everything works like a charm ...

] However, after I begin the installation (basic or
] advanced) and the installation tries to seek out system disks to
] offer me, the machine panics.  

... except the installation process from scratch.  When I connected
the disk to the DECstation I tried to use the generic installation
procedure from CD and booted the installation kernel.  While the
installation system didn't panic on me (using a NeXT CD-drive) it
didn't want to accept the Fujitsu as a system disk either :-(

In my case it is an older release (Ultrix 4.2a).

] I can access the disk from the same machine (booted diskless) and
] everything seems fine.  Is there some way I can prep the disk so
] that it doesn't blow up on install?  

I just booted the original system from the small 200MB disk and
prepared the big one from there -- booting diskless should work for
that as well.  Then I mounted the CDROM in a way that loading the
system with setld worked (which was a bit tricky) and just loaded
everything on the big disk.  As far as I remember I mounted the new
/usr into /newroot/usr so the files would end up where they should go.

The setup for the setld process goes as follows:

I have /export which is the 'h' partition of my big drive.  I mount
the cd into /mnt and have a directory /export/cd which I use as a
base directory for setld.  Despite my expectations, setld coudln't
load the subsets directly from the cd as it wants to have a writable
space to put the logfiles into.

In /export/cd I have a softlink RISC -> /mnt/RISC which points into
the directory where the subsets are really stored on the cd.  I made
a softlink inst -> /usr which points into the installation destination
but this shouldn't be necessary.  While I did the installation it
pointed to /newroot but I relinked it so I can use it now as well.

Now you can use setld -D /newroot -l RISC <subsetname>.


Greetings,
