In article <bickford-0801951235160001@garbanzo.mv.com>, bickford@garbanzo.mv.com (Paul Bickford) writes...
> 
>A simple question:
> 
>A project of mine involves having a VT100 operating without its
>cover on. How safe is this? Besides the danger of dropping paper-
>clips and such inside, how much danger is there from emmisions?
>I imagine it can be too much -- the cover is no more than an
>unshielded piece of plastic, and a VT100 itself is little more
>than a small B&W TV.

In terms of emissions, there is no real danger - the EHT (about 13kV) is too
low to cause hard x-rays, and there is little else in there. The cover does not
do much (if any) screening.

But watch out for high voltages. The card cage area is relatively safe (maximum
voltage about 22 v). BUT, about 1/2 the circuitry on the PSU board (under the
CRT) is not isolated from the power line (it's a switching supply), and in fact
uses about 400V DC, produced by voltage doubling the US power line. The flyback
transformer (bolted to the side of the chassis) generates the EHT for the
tube, and also a few hundred-volt-ish lines for circuitry on the video board.
This board (right side of the tube) is also somewhat dangerous, because of high
voltages on exposed connections.

The VT100 tube is rim-banded, and should not implode violently.

> 
>I just want to be sure that it's not essential to have the cover
>in place before I set up my display using it, that's all.
> 
>Thanks,
> 
>Paul Bickford
>bickford@garbanzo.mv.com

-tony

