In article <bickford-0101951642100001@garbanzo.mv.com>, bickford@garbanzo.mv.com (Paul Bickford) writes:
> Is it possible to 'convert' it to play audio CD's? A big help
> would be if I knew what the pinouts of the 15pin connector
> were.

  The drive is a bare-bones mechanism from LMS (Philips). It's a first-
generation drive and quite slow by any current standards.

  The 15-pin connector is a serial data bus, as I recall. Most of this
drive's limited intelligence is on the controller (I know of a Q-bus and
a PC (ISA) bus controller).

  There isn't any audio circuitry in the drive, so playing audio CD's
would require you to implement enough of the controller circuitry to get
the drive to read, as well as having to build a D/A converter.

  The RRD40 is the following generation Philips/LMS drive and uses weird
caddys. It also doesn't have audio. The SCSI versions of the RRD40 use a
converter from the 15-pin LMS bus to SCSI.

  All of DEC's subsequent CD-ROM drives (RRD42 and later) are based on
"normal" industry drives, usually with modified firmware (the RRD42 has a
512-byte sector size, instead of the usual 2KB, for example).

