Pool fragmentation has been found to be an application dependent
problem that can cause a significant slowdown in throughput. CPU
cycles can be used up in expensive Interrupt Stack Time at IPL 11
and buffered I/O can slow down. Not all systems have this problem,
but the ones that *do* can be helped a lot with MemoryMaster.

The new OpenVMS version 6.x for both AXP and VAX has a new
pool allocation algorithm for non-paged pool and 80 new look-
aside lists. These look aside lists are no longer under control of
the system manager like the earlier IRP, SRP and LRP were.

The new V3.2 MemoryMaster Monitor gives the system manager this
control back and allows monitoring of the failure rates of the look-
aside lists and how much CPU this costs. Fragmentation of non-
paged pool under OVMS 6.x has become *much* higher but
requests for memory packets out of non paged pool are mostly
'caught' by the look aside lists that function as a cache for non
paged pool.

