I used to work with an old LSI-11/2 and an RX02 (even with an RX01!).
The RX02 is a large 19 inch rack mount box which probably dominates
the rack you have. Don't be fooled into thinking that this is the
actual computer. Don't open it, there's nothing in there that might
help you any further. Look at the other parts in that rack. Mine was
a tiny little shoe-box containing 4 or 5 Q-bus dual board slots, the
power supply and little else. It wasn't even half the size of the
RX02 floppy drives and weighed about a fourth of the huge drive
assembly. *That's* the interesting part.
Assuming that the cabling is intact and that there's a controller in
the Q-bus the thing should be bootable, provided you have a bootable
floppy for unit 0 (I believe it was the left hand drive).
If memory serves me right, the @ prompt was called the ODT level
(Online debugger tool or something like that). The stuff I had to type
in to boot it was an address and a letter. Try typing
773000G
Make it a capital "G" and try 173000G if the above doesn't show any
reaction. You don't have to hit Return, the drive's front LED should
react the moment you press the "G".
I ran RT-11 and TSX+ (also known as multiuser-RT-11) with the single
job monitor (a file called RT11SJ.SYS). Worked fine for me back then.
 
Have fun!

