   I'm entering this thread late -- I did not see the previous postings
   referenced by the above posting.  (In other words, this might be a
   duplicate of a previous posting -- if so, please ignore.)

   I'd suggest looking at SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGICALS.COM or (if somebody has
   already edited that file) look in SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGICALS.TEMPLATE.
   One will find a variety of interesting logical names in this file,
   a few of which are the OPCOM-specific logical names...

   This procedure is where all needed-early-in-startup logical names
   should be defined -- if the logical names get defined later along
   in the system startup, they might not be processed as expected.
   This is where the OPCOM-specific logical names are typically placed.

   The more traditional approach of disabling OPCOM on OPA0:

     $ SET PROCESS/PRIV=(OPER,SECURITY)
     $ DEFINE/USER SYS$COMMAND OPA0:
     $ REPLY/DISABLE

   does still work -- but SYLOGICALS is ever-so-slightly cleaner.

