P M Oliver (chppmo@bath.ac.uk) wrote:

: Does anybody have a disktab file for a DEC DSP3160S 1.6Gig drive so that I can
: connect it to a HP 715/33 (HPUX 9).
: I originally tried to find this information from DEC but they wanted to charge
: me for it !! Buying the drive in the first place seemed to have nothing to do 
: with it. (customer support-perhaps not!)

HPUX is not reall fussy about the disk parameters.  It's really only a
performance issue on SCSI.  You don't really need to know about heads
and cylinders and all that.   Use diskinfo to get the size of the disk
and make an entry.  The only thing that really matters is the "s0"
field which is the size of the disk in K bytes.  Set b0 to 8192,
f0 to 1024, and se to 512.  Set rm to 4002.  Make up numbers for
nc, nt, and ns such that ns*nt*nc*512 is the approximage size in
bytes of the disk.

If you are going to use the disk for swap space, then you want to
decrease s0 (the size of the filesystem) by whatever amount you want
to leave for swap.

The only thing that uses this info is newfs.

-Ron



