Previous setup: VAX 4000-200 (hereinafter 4200) with two DSSI disks, one
being the system disk for the LAVC (VMS 5.5-2).

Opportunity: a MicroVAX 3400 no longer needed on a different project,
with three DSSI disks.

Intention: a dual-host configuration (and this is actually one given as
an example in the book) for all the usual server availability reasons.

So I bought the DSSI cable, BC21M-09, and connected the two machines.
The problem is that they won't share the DSSI bus, and the problem
appears at the console prompt level, so I don't believe it's anything to
do with VMS (although it certainly makes VMS behave ... interestingly).

Problem description:

4200 has DIA0 and DIA1; 3400 has DIA2, DIA3, DIA4.  DIA0 is system disk
with SYS0 for the 4200, SYS1 for the 3400.
From the >>> prompt on the 3400 and the 4200,
a) SHOW DSSI on one machine shows all five disks
b) SHOW DSSI on the other machine shows all five disks
c) SHOW DSSI on both machines AT THE SAME TIME shows only some of the
   disks.  I have tried to characterise this further without much joy,
   but it seems as though when a disk responds to one machine it won't be
   seen by the other, so which disks are seen depends on the speed of
   response to the requesting CPU.

Booting VMS: if I boot the 4200, and then boot the 3400, the 4200 loses
connection to the system disk (port closing virtual circuit), and the
3400 never sees the system disk, so everything freezes.  If I shut down
the 4200 and boot the 3400 it comes up OK (i.e the 3400 boots as long as
the 4200 isn't running VMS).  I have to issue an U(NJAM) at both >>>
prompts to get this to work.  If I then boot the 4200, the 3400 loses the
system disk (port closing virtual circuit again), but the 4200 boots and
happily serves the disk to the 3400, so I end up with the 3400 as a
diskless LAVC node (!).  The other nodes see all five DIA disks as served
by the 4200 only (note that if I shut down the 4200 and boot the 3400 and
try to remount the DIA disks from the diskless nodes, they then see them
as served by the 3400, but then I can't bring up the 4200 without freezing).

I don't really need another diskless node, I want a second server !

Due to budget crises I no longer have a hardware maintenance contract,
so I can't just get Digital to come and make it work.  However, there is
some money to fix this, and my first thought was that I needed to buy a
microcode update for the 3400.  Both Digital Field Service and our
self-maintenance technical contacts claim there isn't one.

I know the disks are "smart" (DIA0-3 are RF72, RF73, 3xRF71): is there a
chance I need some sort of update for them ?  Is it the 4200's fault ?
Does anyone have any ideas ??

Plaintive cries for help (I'd include a MIME-compliant audio file, but ...).

