I have the following problem:
 
An UNIX machine (SCO or DG/UX) connected via a TCP/IP link to a VAX
running VMX. These two machines will be transferring work files to each other.
 
FTP will be the transfer method.
 
I would like to devise a notification scheme whereby the arrival of a new
work file causes the machine to kick into action.
 
My experience is with UNIX, I have had some VMS exposure in the past. The
company I am interfacing to uses the VAX strictly as a 4GL application
environment. They are not familar with the low level VMS system routines.
 
I have already thought of the following schemes:
 
polling:	
 
periodically poll the spool directory. This is a simple but bad
solution in that it wastes CPU time on both machines.
 
run a remote program via rshell and rcmd:
 
This works quite well but rcmd and rshell can take a long time to execute.
In tests that I have done, it's taken up to 10 seconds for a simple
rshell command to run.
 
Use ftp to send a file to a named pipe:
 
This works well for the Unix side, but ftp will hang if no process is reading
the named pipe. Also I don't know if "named pipes (FIFOS)" exist
in VMS.
 
Can anyone suggest a notification scheme that consists of two processes
being blocked (one on each machine) that unblock when a file is transferred,
or deleted, or permissions are changed.
 
This must be a fairly common problem and I'm sure an elegant solution exists.
 
Any help will be appreciated.
 
Cary Lewis


