In article <D1y2Ew.981@hplabsz.hpl.hp.com>, perry@hpl.hp.com
(Jeff Perry) writes:

|>I was wondering if it's possible to setup an AlphaStation to dual-boot
|>OpenVMS and WinNT. What I am thinking of is something like the capability
|>PC's have to boot either DOS or NT. 
|>
|>Does anyone know if this can be done? If so, how would you go about
|>configuring the system? Do you install OpenVMS and then NT?. Has anyone
|>had any success (or failures) doing this?

   One can boot NT, OSF/1 or OpenVMS on various 2000 and 2100-series
   Alpha systems.

   To configure a system for this, one will typically want multiple disks
   (one or more per OS).  One could also use just one disk -- requiring
   one to unload and reload the disk -- but that can rapidly get tedious
   if swapping is a frequent operation.

   To boot, one needs to load the appropriate PALcode -- OSF/1 and OpenVMS
   share the same PALcode and console (SRM), while NT has its own PALcode
   and console (ARC).  (There are a couple of simple console commands
   invoked here.  These steps are often on the order of "set os_type nt"
   (or vms), power cycle to force the reload of the PALcode, and boot.)
   These steps are documented in the processor-specific manual and in the
   PALcode manuals.

   Given the choice, I'd install NT first.

