[...]
#run a remote program via rshell and rcmd:
# 
#This works quite well but rcmd and rshell can take a long time to execute.
#In tests that I have done, it's taken up to 10 seconds for a simple
#rshell command to run.

Add code like:

  $ IF F$MODE() .EQS. "OTHER" THEN EXIT

near the top of both the SYS$SYLOGIN (usually SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM) and the
user's login file (usually SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM).  That will bypass all the
(likely unnecessary stuff) and get the RSHELL command to complete a bit
faster.

#Use ftp to send a file to a named pipe:
# 
#This works well for the Unix side, but ftp will hang if no process is reading
#the named pipe. Also I don't know if "named pipes (FIFOS)" exist
#in VMS.

Nope.

#Can anyone suggest a notification scheme that consists of two processes
#being blocked (one on each machine) that unblock when a file is transferred,
#or deleted, or permissions are changed.
# 
#This must be a fairly common problem and I'm sure an elegant solution exists.

RSHELL is probably the best solution; you could simply do the FTP from the
Unix side, then a simple RSHELL to the VMS system which SUBMITs a batch job
that does work on the VMS system to move the file out of the spool area, or
whatever needs to be done -- that way the VMS system could do the work without
tying up the Unix side.

